    content: `Chopsticks are the primary eating utensil across much of East Asia, but their exact shape and associated etiquette vary meaningfully by country. Chinese chopsticks are typically longer and have blunt, often square-ish tips, traditionally made of wood, bamboo, or (for special occasions) more elaborate materials. Japanese chopsticks are usually shorter, tapered to a fine point (useful for the delicate task of deboning fish, a much more common part of everyday Japanese cuisine), and often lacquered. Korean chopsticks are distinctively made of flat, thin metal, historically originating in part from royal court customs and now standard for everyday use, and are typically used alongside a spoon rather than eating rice directly with the chopsticks, unlike common practice in China and Japan. A widely shared etiquette rule across all three cultures is to never stick chopsticks vertically upright into a bowl of rice, since the resulting image closely resembles incense sticks placed in a bowl of sand or ash at a funeral or ancestral altar, making the gesture a genuine, sometimes serious, cultural faux pas rather than simply an odd or messy habit.`,
