    content: `Tipping expectations differ dramatically by country, and travelers who assume their home country's norms apply everywhere can easily cause offense or draw unwanted attention in either direction. In the United States, tipping (commonly 15-20% at sit-down restaurants) is a deeply embedded, near-mandatory practice, since many service workers historically earn a lower base wage explicitly on the assumption that tips will make up a significant portion of their income. In Japan, tipping is generally not practiced and can even be considered mildly awkward or confusing to service staff, since excellent, attentive service is culturally viewed as simply the standard, expected baseline rather than something requiring extra financial reward — attempting to leave a tip in many Japanese establishments may prompt a server to politely chase down a customer to return the "forgotten" money. Across much of Europe, service charges are frequently already included in the bill, and any additional tipping tends to be far more modest than American norms, often just rounding up the bill or leaving small change rather than a large percentage. In several countries, particularly across parts of the Middle East and Asia, small tips are appreciated but far from an ingrained obligation. Because norms vary so widely, checking specific local customs (or reading the bill carefully for an already-included service charge) before traveling is generally more reliable than assuming any single global standard.`,
