    content: `A tonal language is one in which the pitch or pitch contour used to pronounce a syllable is not just an expressive or emotional cue (as it typically is in English) but can fundamentally change a word's actual meaning, making tone as essential to correct pronunciation as consonants and vowels themselves. Mandarin Chinese, one of the most widely known tonal languages, has four main tones (plus a neutral tone) — the same syllable "ma," for example, can mean "mother," "hemp," "horse," or "scold," depending entirely on whether it's spoken with a flat high tone, a rising tone, a dipping-then-rising tone, or a sharply falling tone. Tonal languages are found across many language families worldwide, including most Chinese languages, Vietnamese, Thai, and a large number of African languages, particularly across the Niger-Congo language family, which includes Yoruba and Igbo. Non-tonal languages, including English, Spanish, and most European languages, still use pitch expressively (rising intonation to indicate a question, for instance) but changing pitch on an individual word generally doesn't change its core dictionary meaning the way it reliably does in a genuinely tonal language, which is part of why tonal languages can be especially challenging for adult learners whose native language doesn't use pitch this way.`,
