    content: `Writing systems around the world are generally categorized into a few broad types based on what a single written symbol represents. An alphabetic system, like the Latin alphabet used for English and many European languages, or the Cyrillic alphabet used for Russian, uses individual symbols to represent individual sounds (phonemes), generally including both consonants and vowels. A syllabary uses individual symbols to represent whole syllables rather than individual sounds — Japanese uses two such syllabaries, hiragana and katakana, each containing roughly 46 basic characters, generally used respectively for native Japanese words/grammar and for foreign loanwords/emphasis. A logographic system uses individual symbols (logograms) to represent entire words or meaningful units of meaning rather than sounds — Chinese characters (hanzi, also used historically and to varying extents today in Japanese as kanji and formerly in Korean as hanja) are the most prominent living example, with thousands of distinct characters in regular use, each generally representing a word or meaningful concept rather than being sounded out phonetically from its individual components in the way an alphabet works. Japanese writing is unusual in regularly combining all three approaches — kanji, hiragana, and katakana — together within the same sentence, each serving a different grammatical or stylistic function.`,
