    content: `An antipodal point (or "antipode") is the location on the exact opposite side of the Earth from a given point, found mathematically by taking a location's latitude and flipping its sign (north becomes south, or vice versa) while adding 180 degrees to its longitude. Because roughly 71% of Earth's surface is ocean, most points on land do not actually have a corresponding point of land directly on their antipode — the popular idea of "digging a hole straight through the Earth" from a given country and coming out in some specific other named country is true for very few real-world pairs of locations; most land-based points antipodally correspond to open ocean rather than another populated landmass. One well-known accurate example is that parts of Spain lie roughly antipodal to parts of New Zealand, and Argentina lies roughly antipodal to parts of China's northeast, though even these examples generally require some approximation since exact antipodal precision is rare between two specific inhabited cities. Practically speaking, actually reaching your antipode by digging straight down is also physically impossible for reasons far beyond distance — Earth's mantle and outer core reach temperatures and pressures no known material or vehicle could survive.`,
