    content: `While most of the world's time zones are offset from Coordinated Universal Time (UTC) in whole-hour increments, a number of countries deliberately use unusual half-hour or even 45-minute offsets. India uses a single time zone nationwide, UTC+5:30, chosen as a compromise roughly midway between the country's easternmost and westernmost longitudes to keep the entire country administratively unified on one clock despite its considerable east-west geographic width. Nepal uses an even more unusual offset of UTC+5:45, and a handful of other regions, including parts of Australia, also use 30-minute offsets rather than whole hours. China is geographically wide enough to naturally span roughly five standard time zones based on longitude alone, yet the entire country officially uses a single time zone, Beijing Time (UTC+8), for national administrative unity — a decision that means far-western regions of China experience sunrise and sunset at clock times dramatically different from what their actual longitude would otherwise suggest compared to eastern China. France, despite being a relatively modest-sized country in mainland Europe, technically has more time zones than any other country in the world once its various overseas territories (spread across the Pacific, Caribbean, and Indian Ocean) are included.`,
