    content: `Vatican City, at roughly 0.44 square kilometers, is the smallest sovereign state in the world by both land area and population, functioning as an independent city-state entirely surrounded by Rome, Italy, and serving as the seat of the Catholic Church under the Pope. Monaco, a small city-state on the French Riviera, is the second-smallest country by area but among the most densely populated in the world, well known for the Monaco Grand Prix Formula 1 race run through its city streets and for its status as a wealthy tax haven and gambling destination (notably the Monte Carlo Casino). San Marino, entirely surrounded by Italy, claims to be the oldest surviving sovereign state and constitutional republic in the world, tracing its founding to the early 4th century according to tradition. Nauru, a tiny Pacific island nation, is the smallest independent island nation in the world and one of the smallest countries overall, with an economy historically dependent on phosphate mining that has since been largely depleted, leaving the country facing significant long-term economic and environmental challenges. These microstates each maintain full UN-recognized sovereignty despite their small size, generally sustained through specialized economic niches like finance, tourism, or religious significance rather than large-scale industry or agriculture.`,
